byron@cc.gatech.edu (Byron A Jeff) wrote:

> I'm adding some space before some rather severe SPOILERS....

You're still reading this thread? Then you're
reading it for the spoilers, so I won't.

> 1. In the early game, STOP KILLING MONSTERS! ...
> The only exception in the early game are newts,
> because they may give you a point or two of extra
> power when eaten. The kills only cost 1 point
> each.

If you lurk next to your pet, you can sometimes
displace a pet away from a corpse in the
(occasional) one turn before the pet is eating the
corpse, or, if you just love newt nibbles, stand
next to the newt and try to dive directly onto the
corpse before your pet can. This only works
sometimes, but it saves you a few XP. You don't,
especially, need MP at this stage anyway; you're
probably only casting spells for added spellcasting
experience, and as noted here earlier in another
thread, you don't need to cast, say, force bolt at
anything more important than a wall to be procticing
it, so you don't need to gain XP in its use.

If you are playing the protection racket/credit
clone combination, you'll be spending _lots_ of time
in the first four levels and the parts of mines down
to mines town, you don't need spell points back
instantly, they'll come, anyway.

> 2. Gather money. Sell everything you can afford at
> the shop.

Spellbooks are great for this. You won't need your
starting ones for 20,000 turns, and they usually
sell at a very good price. Later, as a rich and
powerful wizard growing absent minded about just how
a spell was worded, you can return and buy them
back, or, if some monster wanders off from the shop
with them, find them elsewhere on the level.

> If you personally are of other than lawful
> alignment for game playing then invest in credit
> cloning (look in google groups for the term)

Unless you're role playing heavily, IIUC, credit
cloning isn't a problem for lawfuls, either, at
least within the game mechanics. All the stealing is
done by your pet, not you. All of _your_
transactions are legal ones.

> 3. Don't be afraid to wear armor in the early
> game. You're being passive which means that you
> won't have to cast a whole lot of spells.

Right, and the better your armor, the safer it is to
stand right next to a pet who is in melee, to try to
grab the corpse of its kill, before it can.

_Do_ be careful about wearing armor so heavy you
can't carry minimal gear without being burdened,
though. A wizard in plate mail is probably always
a bad idea, except with 25 strength.

> 4. ...

> One issue to be careful of: a maxed out cat loves
> to snack on the Minetown guards. Unfortunately the
> watch captain is usually strong enough to take the
> cat out.

It can work to lure the watch captain into a room
and lock him/her in their, if you have a key, a
lockpick (or wizard lock, I guess), and s/he doesn't
have a key. This can really prolong the life of a
starting dog or cat pet. Even the minestown guards
can kill your pets with a couple of lucky hits, so
be ready to use a leash, to displace a pet from
melee, or to leave Mines Town gathering XPs as you
go, doing your own fighting.

> 5. Now you're in midgame. A low AC and a strong
> pet will carry you a long long way.

Since you've broken atheist conduct long ago, are
praying for food and so don't really have to care
how long it takes to do a level, this can be a good
time to do lower level Mines, on a "search, search,
step" basis, trying to find a polymorph trap before
it finds you. Once found, your pet can suddenly get
a lot more impressive by stepping into it. Like the
earlier levels, this can be a _lot_ safer if you
keep your XL low.

> Now it's time to start spellcasting. Ditch as much
> of the metal armor as you can at this point.

For a wizard, priest, or healer, this is most of the
point of the protection racket: being able to wear
less armor so spellcasting works better.

